chunks.concept:
A **plane (flat) mirror** forms an image that is:

- **virtual** — the light never actually goes behind the mirror; it only appears to;
- **upright** — the same way up as the object;
- **the same size** as the object (magnification 1);
- **laterally reversed** — left and right appear swapped (why text looks mirror-written);
- located **as far behind the mirror as the object is in front**, directly opposite along the perpendicular to the mirror.

Because the image is the same distance behind as the object is in front, if you move **toward** the mirror at speed $v$, your image moves toward the mirror at $v$ too, so the **gap between you and your image closes at $2v$**.

The minimum mirror height to see your whole body is **half your height**, $H/2$, hung correctly — and this does not depend on how far away you stand.

chunks.representation:
Plane mirror image: **virtual, upright, same size**, distance behind = distance in front, left-right reversed.

- move toward mirror at $v$ → you approach your image at $2v$;
- full-body mirror needs height $\ge H/2$.
